t1 Compilation: Globals

t2 ZAEs Organization in compiler

t3 Scope parsing logic

p The global scope is the main scope and the \uhighest\u in our program; it \
has no parent. User code \uis\u in that global scope by default. Therefore, \
our unparsed ZCI list we have as entry point is defining instructions in \
\uglobal scope\u.

p The only way to create local scopes is to create \ufunctions\u, using \
\mDCL_FCT\m ZCIs which must be declared, in all cases, in \uglobal scope\u. \
Therefore, we must parse global scope ZCIs \bbefore\b local ones.

p By the way, because functions are declared as global ZCIs, their internal \
scope naturally has the global one as parent, and so, they are able to access \
to global ZAEs.

p This same logic applies for scopes declared inside functions (using \
statements for example): Local scopes always have access to their parent's \
content (upper scopes). As a result, we must parse \uhigher scopes\u first, \
and then finish with the lowest ones.


t3 Storing ZAEs

p Using that scope parsing logic, everything in a Z code can be stored using, \
for example, a tree like this:
"
CPL_instance
└── gbl_scp
    │
    ├── Type     tG1
    ├── Type     tG2
    ├── DataItem dG3
    ├── DataItem dG4
    │
    ├── Function A
    │   └── lcl_scp A
    │       ├── DatItm dA1
    │       ├── Exe    eA2
    │       └── Exe    eA3
    │
    ├── Function B
    │   └── lcl_scp B
    │       ├── DatItm    dB1
    │       ├── Statement eB2
    │       │   └── lcl_scp eB2
    │       │       ├── Exe eB2.e1
    │       │       └── Exe eB2.e2
    │       ├── Exe eB3
    │       └── Exe eB4
    │
    └── Function C
        └── lcl_scp C
            ├── DatItm dC1
            └── Exe    eC2
"

p There is a big difference between the global scope and local ones. The \
global scope is aim to be a \udefinition place\u to declare \ustatic\u things \
(types, functions, process-static memory items...). It \ucan't have dynamic\u \
things like local scope executions or any runtime dependant resources \
(function calls, statements...).

p Because of this, in ZINC, all the things that are global scope specific are \
not stored in it, but directly in the main compiler instance. Scope objects \
are mainly used for local purpose.

p They contain data items (static memory usage for the lifetime of the scope) \
and their executions (none for the global one) only. Of course, they still \
have access to their parent scope to use their declared data items. The tree \
structure looks more like this:
"
CPL_instance
├── types
│   ├── Type tG1
│   └── Type tG2
│
├── gbl_scp
│   ├── DataItems
│   │   ├── dG3
│   │   └── dG4
│   └── Exes
│
└── functions
    ├── Function A
    │   └── lcl_scp A
    │       ├── DataItems
    │       │   └── dA1
    │       └── Exes
    │           ├── eA2
    │           └── eA3
    │
    ├── Function B
    │   └── lcl_scp B
    │       ├── DataItems
    │       │   └── dB1
    │       └── Exes
    │           ├── eB2
    │           │   └── lcl_scp eB2
    │           │       ├── DataItems
    │           │       └── Exes
    │           │           ├── eB2.e1
    │           │           └── eB2.e1
    │           ├── eB3
    │           └── eB4
    │
    └── Function C
        └── lcl_scp C
            ├── DataItems
            │   └── dC1
            └── Exes
                └── eC2
"

p This way, scope related ZAEs can rely on generic elements such as types and \
functions, non-scope ZAEs. Therefore, it seems logic that we should parse them \
first, skipping every other ZCI for the moment. However, there is still one \
kind of ZAE to take into account before starting global parsing: Z modules.



t2 C1 - Unmodulize

t3 Objective

p Z modules are an abstract representation of code that can contain both \
global and local ZAEs (function declarations). However, every ZCI defined in a \
module is contained in a braces includer, which (as a recall) stays unparsed \
as \uraw text\u in the module declaration ZCI.

p As a consequence, to know whether a global ZCI is contained in a module, we \
must parse them first. This is why the first compilation step is called \
\bunmodulize\b. A module is nothing more than a couple of ZCIs where every \
declaration is to be preceeded by the given module \uprefix\u.

p The objective here is to replace every module declaration ZCI \mDCL_MOD\m in \
our ZCI list by all the ZCIs we could extract from the module. Also, these \
extracted ZCIs must keep the information of the module they were taken from, \
for further parsing steps.

p Also, Z modules can contain other sub-modules in themselves. Thus, we must \
also check the resulted extracted ZCIs to decompose every module completely. \
As a result, because modules must be declared in \uglobal scope\u, it should \
not have any remaining \mDCL_MOD\m ZCI in the code, and the current list of \
ZCIs to be parsed are all and only global scope related.


t3 Unmodulizing

p First we will look for module declaration ZCI using the \mmod\m keyword. It \
can be a module declaration (new module) or a module addition (module must \
already exist). Then, taking all the text defined between the module braces
includer, we extract global ZCIs from it using the same mechanism as in \
precompiler step P3.

p To keep track of module prefixing throughout submodule declarations, ZCI \
objects contains in themselves a \umodule prefix\u text, empty by default. \
When module ZCIs are extracted, the resulted ZCIs are initialized with the \
module name \badded at the end\b of their \umodule prefix\u (modPfx \m""\m \
=> \m"^MyModule."\m).

p Then, the current ZCI for module declaration/addition is removed and the \
extracted ZCIs are inserted at its position instead. We can now continue the \
unmodulization process on the newly extracted ZCIs.

p Naturally, if we have submodule declarations, the module prefix of inner \
ZCIs will be completed accordingly to the Z modularization logic: modPfx \
\m""\m => \m"^A."\m => \m"^A.^SubA."\m => \m"^A.^SubA.^SubSubA."\m => ...

p \uNote:\u If compilation option \mATM_GENERATED_CONTENT\m is enabled, some \
atom elements will be generated in an \mAtm\m module so you will not be able \
to declare a module with that same name.




t2 C2 - Redirect Globals

t3 Objective

p Since we now have all the global ZCIs of our program, we should be able to \
parse them all directly without using additional steps. Though compiler code \
is split in several parts because of the amount of complexity in that step, \
this task is made at once, and result in having parsed \bevery\b global ZCIs.

p Each ZCI is analyzed and will be parsed according to its content. The use of \
\uspecific\u and \ushort\u keywords at the beginning helps a lot for ZCI \
identification. If one global ZCI does not match any of the allowed patterns, \
an error "Unknown ZCI" is raised, ZCS is inconsistent.


t3 Redirections

l Here is how global redirection is made:
"
│   #forbidden cases
├── "imp" keyword
│   └── INT ERR "Must not have remaining importation ZCIs after step P3"
│
├── "brk/ctn/ret/if/elf/els/for/whi/swi" keyword
│   └── ERR "ZCI only allowed in local scope"
│
│
│
│   #keyword based
├── "pub/prv" keyword
│   └── Set access modifier for that ZCI, but continue parsing
│
├── "asu" keyword
│   └── Parse as EXT_ASU ZCI
│
├── "typ" keyword
│   └── Parse as DCL_TYP ZCI
│
├── "enm" keyword
│   └── Parse as DCL_ENM ZCI (gbl)
│
├── "fct" keyword
│   └── Parse as DCL_FCT ZCI
│
├── "fwd" keyword
│   └── Parse as DCL_FWD ZCI
│
├── "cst" keyword
│   └── Parse as DCL_DAT ZCI (gbl)
│
│
│
│   #non-keyword
├── Remaining cases
│   ├── Starts with a VALUE ZAE
│   │   ├── Followed by ASG symbol
│   │   │   └── Parse as ASG_ASG ZCI (gbl)
│   │   │
│   │   └── Parse as VFC_VFC ZCI (gbl => will raise ERR)
│   │
│   └── Can only be a DCL_DAT ZCI (gbl) with explicit type given
│
│
│
│   #doesn't match any of the known cases
└── Unknown ZCI
    └── ERR "Unknown ZCI (Invalid ZCS)"
"

p In all of these specific treatments, ZCIs containing local scopes will keep \
them aside, unparsed, as a list of raw ZCIs to be analyzed later. They are \
extracted using the same process as step P3 and C1.



t2 C2 - EXT_ASU

t3 General parsing

p EXT_ASU ZCIs only have one field to parse: the external resource path. This \
one can be either relative from the location of the source code invoking it, \
or absolute. No extension is required, or will be added if missing for the \
targetted file: it must be complete.

p If an external resource is loaded twice, it will be skipped silently (still \
visible using debug traces though). To process the "external assumed code" \
footprint file, we parse it as a \u\bstandard\b\u \lhttps://github.com/iasebsil83/Config\lConfig\l \
file first, and then we go into parsing the names given and their associated \
values.

p As a recall, we can only have \btypes\b, \bfunctions\b and \bglobal data \
items\b defined in external footprints. To ensure a consistent compilation, we \
need to load these external assumed ZAEs among the others. However, it is \
\bhighly important\b to remember that they come from an \uexternal\u resource.

p To know whether a type, function or data item is external or not, we add an \
\mext\m attribute in the data structure we use for storing these ZAEs in \
compiler code tree.



t2 C2 - DCL_TYP

t3 General parsing

p The first thing we do when parsing type declaration is to look for the given \
new type name and check whether an instance with that type name already exists \
or not. Note that we look for an \uundeclinated\u instance only (can't declare \
a "declinated" type) and we take into consideration the ZCI module prefix in \
the type name targetted.

p If everything is okay, we declare the undeclinated instance with the correct \
declination degree, and respecting the 2 declaration syntaxes: \utype copy\u \
or \ustructure definition\u. Every inner types used in fields must be already \
declared \bbefore\b being used (structure definition syntax).


t3 Atoms

p If compilation option \mATM_GENERATED_CONTENT\m is enabled, a constant \
\m^Atm.typeName\m will be created alongside with a method \mtypeName.toAtm()\m \
for converting an instance of that type into an atm (of course, the \
\mtypeName\m used corresponds to the \bfull\b type name (including module \
prefixes).
" z Example
typ alpha ...
mod M { typ beta ... }

//atm generated constants
^Atm.alpha
^Atm.^M.beta

//atm generated methods
alpha   a
^M.beta b
a_atm = a.toAtm()
b_atm = b.toAtm()

//generated methods corresponds to this:
fct alpha.toAtm() {
	ret atm{ id=^Atm.alpha, dat=@slf }
}
fct ^M.beta.toAtm() {
	ret atm{ id=^Atm.^M.beta, dat=@slf }
}
"

p This compilation option also assume type \matm\m as being an external \
resource, as if it was given in a footprint file using EXT_ASU ZCI.



t2 C2 - DCL_ENM

t3 General parsing

p Enumerate declaration creates a type with the given enumerate name. Fields \
are parsed as data items, however they can't have a value or type associated. \
As a result, no types are given to enumerate fields; we set them afterwards. \
Depending on the amount of fields we have in the enumerate, we are giving the \
type \mu8\m, \mu16\m, \mu32\m or \mu64\m (minimum size required is being used).

p When refering to an enumerate value (in 2nd analysis), we will refer to the \
fields of that created type. The resulted value \utype\u will then be the one \
of the \ufield\u and not the enumerate type itself.



t2 C2 - DCL_FCT & DCL_FWD

t3 General parsing

p Function declaration and forwarding are 2 ways of declaring a function in Z. \
They have a big common part in the parsing process that creates, in both \
cases, a \mfct\m instance with all the basic informations: is it related to a \
type (method) ? is it dcn-dependant ? is it an operator ? Compute full name, \
check parameters and add them as first data items in function local scope.

p Return type is parsed afterwards because forwarded functions requires the \
source function name to be given before. In regular function declaration, the \
content given in braces includer is extracted as raw ZCIs to be parsed later \
(local scope), and for function forwarding, no content is expected, the result \
is directly generated as already parsed local scope execution.

l Forwarded functions produce the generated equivalent Z code:
" z Forwarded functions execution
//some types
typ alpha ...
typ beta  ...
typ gamma ...

//some functions
fct alpha.myFunc(boo p1, chr p2) s32 { ... }
fct beta.myFuncV(u64 p1, s16 p2)     { ... }

//some forwardings
fwd gamma alpha.myFunc(boo p1, byt p2) u32
fwd gamma beta.myFuncV(s64 p1, u16 p2)

//equivalent Z code for these forwardings
fct gamma.myFunc(boo p1, byt p2) u32 {
	ret slf$alpha.myFunc(p1, p2)
}
fct gamma.myFuncV(s64 p1, u16 p2) {
	slf$beta.myFuncV(p1, p2)
}
"



t2 C2 - DCL_DAT

t3 General parsing

p When ZCI is detected as DCL_DAT ZCI, process is simple: we parse it as a \
data item declaration: optional type name including module prefixes, raw data \
item name, optional initial value that can also solve implicit type given. \
Then we add it to the targetted scope. Even if initial value is already stored \
in the data item itself, we create an \masg\m execution for that initial value \
in the given scope too (will be useful later).



t2 C2 - ASG_ASG

t3 General parsing

p When arriving to this kind of ZCI parsing, we already have parsed the first \
assigment value: the destination, and we went after the assignment symbol. So \
we only have to parse the source value to assign and then parsing may be \
completed: we should just have to create an \masg\m instance as execution to \
add in the targetted scope (the global one here).

p Nevertheless, in Z, we can't assign into any kind of value (can't assign \
into a literal value for example). Moreover, some "assignment-like" ZCIs also \
have special behaviors such as when assigning into indexed value (IIA, ISA \
operators). In these last cases, the resulted ZCI is not even an ASG_ASG but a \
VFC_VFC.


t3 Cases

p That's why before creating an \masg\m instance, we first look at the \
destination value we have. If it is a call to an IIA or ISA operator (no \
matter the parameters), we process them as a VFC with the source value added \
as last parameter.

p If the destination value wasn't a call but a data item (just a targettable \
name given to assign into), we are having a regular assignment. Actually, \
there is still one more case that could have been found: if it was an \
\uimplicit declaration\u ?

p Not a big deal, we could just check if a data item with that name exists in \
current scope or higher, and so declare locally if not. \bBUT\b, we don't even \
require to do this in our case, because the second analysis mechanism we used \
to get our ASG_ASG values never returns a "data item" value if not available \
(already declared).

p If we have an implicit declaration, second analysis will then return an \
empty value because the name given doesn't target an existing data item. So, \
as you can see in the previous schema of global redirections, the ZCI will be \
directly redirected as being a DCL_DAT => problem solved.



b
